# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# cloudflare / opennext
# .open-next is the build output - the equivalent of .next for this adapter.
/.open-next/
# wrangler's local state (dev registry, sqlite, cached types). It appeared as an
# untracked directory the first time `opennextjs-cloudflare preview` ran.
#
# NOT anchored to the root, and that is load-bearing. A second wrangler config exists
# under cron/, and miniflare resolves its persistence directory relative to the CONFIG
# FILE rather than to the repository - so running the cron worker locally creates
# cron/.wrangler/state/v3/ and a root-anchored pattern does not cover it. The visible
# symptom of getting this wrong is a local database with no tables.
.wrangler/
# Local Worker variables. The committed template is .dev.vars.example; this file is
# ignored because it is where a real secret would eventually be put.
.dev.vars
.dev.vars.*
!.dev.vars.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Python bytecode. scripts/report/render-report.py is imported by the geometry negative-control
# check, and importing it writes this directory next to the source.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Generated client reports - scripts/report/build-report.mts writes here by default.
#
# IGNORED ON PURPOSE, and not only to keep the tree tidy. These files name a client's domain, quote
# their page content and carry their scores, and this repository is public: anything committed here
# is published whether or not a page renders it. That is the same reason OPERATIONS.md gives for
# why an Impressum address could not live in this repository.
/reports/
